# Story: someone holds ⌘ and drags windows about, in passing and then meaning it; then lets go of the key and only moves them.
# The simulated display is 1440x900 with a 25-point menu bar.
# Reference: T10 T01 T02. Spec: F09.
# The three tiling switches of the guide's section 2 are off: with macOS's own tiling on, the left, right and top edges of the screen are macOS's (Q23).
# As the first build shipped it: docked windows are dragged out onto the Desktop beside others.
set desktopColumn true
mac-tiling off
open Safari
open Notes
open Mail
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
# Mail sweeps over the bottom of Notes and is let go without stopping: it floats.
drag 103 1200 850 quick cmd
expect floating 103
expect count docked 2
# Held there for a moment, it docks below Notes.
drag 103 1200 850 cmd
expect docked 103
expect above 102 103
# Safari, which is docked, sweeps over Mail and is let go without stopping: it goes back to its slot.
drag 101 1200 850 quick cmd
expect docked 101
expect left-of 101 102
expect count docked 3
# Let go over the middle of Notes, where nothing is previewed, it goes back too, held there or not.
drag 101 1200 250 quick cmd
expect docked 101
drag 101 1200 250 cmd
expect docked 101
expect left-of 101 102
expect count docked 3
# With ⌘ held only the Desktop takes a docked window out of the arrangement, and back it goes.
drag 101 720 450 quick cmd
expect floating 101
expect count docked 2
drag 101 3 450 cmd
expect docked 101
expect left-of 101 102
# A screen edge does not need holding.
drag 103 720 28 quick cmd
expect docked 103
expect above 103 101
expect above 103 102
# Without the Command key nothing docks: not at a screen edge, not on a window.
drag 103 3 450
expect floating 103
drag 103 200 850
expect floating 103
# A plain drag also takes a docked window out, even when it is let go over its own slot.
drag 101 250 400
expect floating 101
expect count docked 1
